WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.350
In a world where freedom is history.

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:04.970
In a world without gas.

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:07.450
In a world that's powered by violence.

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:09.730
Narrator: These days,
people get just as excited

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:13.160
for a movie trailer as
they do the actual movie.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:16.160
Chris Pratt: I brought
a clip, like a preview.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:17.320
Jimmy Kimmel: Oh, really?

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.360
Narrator: Trailers can
make or break a film,

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.140
and they even have their own award show.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:24.973
Matt Brubaker: They almost become 

00:00:24.973 --> 00:00:26.264
their own entertainment form.

00:00:26.264 --> 00:00:27.900
Narrator: That's Matt Brubaker,

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.560
CEO of Trailer Park in L.A.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:31.800
His company is responsible

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.650
for some of the biggest film
trailers in the past few years,

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:36.183
including "Wonder Woman"

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:38.953
"Baby Driver"

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:41.503
- I'm a driver.

00:00:41.503 --> 00:00:43.980
Narrator: and "Mad Max," which
won a Golden Trailer award

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:45.780
for Best Original Trailer.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.680
So what goes into an
award winning trailer?

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.130
Brubaker: Oftentimes, trailers are started

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:53.580
while filming is being done.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.340
Narrator: The entire
process can take anywhere

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:57.580
from two weeks to two years.

00:00:57.580 --> 00:00:59.010
The earlier, the better,

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.710
so they can be ready for hyped
up events like Comic-Con.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.470
Brubaker: So when we start a trailer,

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:06.160
we get the direction call from the studio.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:08.640
Then we have a creative
meeting internally.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.040
There's 10 to 15 people
working on one project.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:13.350
Narrator: This includes editors,

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.490
assistant editors, coordinators,

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:18.420
writers, composers, and
a creative director.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.130
On big projects, sometimes
up to six different companies

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:23.540
could all be contracted to work on a film.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:25.580
Brubaker: Some features
we start with dailies,

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.619
which, you know, are the daily of footage

00:01:27.619 --> 00:01:29.970
that a filmmaker shoots.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:32.060
Narrator: And since the
movie is still being shot,

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:34.100
some of this footage
might never even appear

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:35.270
in the actual movie.

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:36.740
Brubaker: Sometimes
we're working with things

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:39.030
that don't end up in the final feature,

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:40.110
and vice versa.

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.700
It's not that we're trying
to trick the viewer.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.840
It's more of we're
having to tell the story

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:45.720
in two and a half minutes,

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:47.160
not in three hours.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:48.370
Narrator: There are usually restrictions

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:50.550
on what can be revealed in a trailer.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:52.160
Oftentimes, there's a plan in place

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:53.790
to gradually reveal plot points

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:56.390
and characters through
different campaigns.

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:58.510
Brubaker: Like when it comes
to high security movies,

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:00.410
like Marvel movies,

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:02.880
it's part of the creative
directive of what we can show

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:04.030
and what we can't show.

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:07.650
With movies like "The Avengers"
and other Marvel campaigns,

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.910
there might be three or
four trailer campaigns.

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:12.581
Narrator: And the whole
process is on lockdown

00:02:12.581 --> 00:02:14.130
to prevent leaks.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:16.100
Brubaker: It's like Fort Knox.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:17.200
There's cameras everywhere.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.010
There's like, you can't have internet

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:21.940
at your workstation for edit bays.

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:23.720
Everything is a fireable offense,

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:28.720
and the MPA has put in
effect a very strict policy

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:30.560
of how we handle materials.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:34.560
- "Star Wars," a billion
years in the making,

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.933
and it's coming to your
galaxy this summer.

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:40.160
Narrator: Movie trailers have evolved

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:41.290
a lot over the years.

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:42.830
One trick that used to be very common

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:43.970
is quick cuts.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:45.208
Brubaker: There was a
time where quick cutting

00:02:45.208 --> 00:02:49.030
might represent a lot
of energy and action.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:52.550
Now, I think that a lot of
people want to hear a story,

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:55.120
and want to know and see

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:57.060
if the movie actually stands on its own.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.260
Narrator: These days, you're
likely to see more dialogue

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.910
and plot details in a movie trailer.

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:04.730
You've also probably noticed more teasers,

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:07.560
which are basically a
trailer to a trailer.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.660
Teasers tease the concept of a movie,

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:11.350
while showing very little footage.

00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:13.703
Brubaker: "The Social
Network" had a great teaser.

00:03:14.779 --> 00:03:17.500
"The Girl with the Dragon
Tattoo" had a great teaser.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:20.270
It was something that whetted
the audience's appetite.

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:22.720
Narrator: Music is also
key to a good trailer.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:28.013
- He looks heroic,

00:03:28.891 --> 00:03:30.740
but he's kinda got a negative attitude.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:32.506
Brubaker: I think music and sound design 

00:03:32.506 --> 00:03:33.598
is always important,

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:35.390
and I think having it be more unique

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:39.320
and special to a property is in vogue.

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:41.550
Narrator: Trailer Park has
two internal composers,

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:43.240
who create music for the trailers.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:47.150
Brubaker: There's a trend
now for taking old cues

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:48.610
and redoing them,

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:50.250
and we do that a lot,

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:53.763
and customize cues for trailers.

00:03:58.162 --> 00:03:59.440
Narrator: There can be
many different versions

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.630
of a trailer before the
final one is released.

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:03.510
One trailer can go through hundreds

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:05.370
of different versions of edits.

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:10.350
Brubaker: We've had trailers
go through past version 200.

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:13.560
Generally, it's the studio
who gets the final sign off.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:14.470
Narrator: And with some many people

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:16.580
sharing these videos on social media,

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:19.840
a trailer is crucial to a
film's box office success.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:22.670
Brubaker: Really, the first
time a studio and the world

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:25.110
sees anything is through the trailer,

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:26.660
and it's increasingly more important,

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:28.020
and there's such a focus

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:31.210
and such a magnifying glass to what we do.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:33.480
Narrator: So some studios
will focus group the trailers

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:34.650
to make sure they work.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:36.330
Brubaker: If you see a
trailer that goes out

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:37.820
that just makes the movie

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:39.430
look questionable,

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:42.712
it's deadly for the
property to overcome that.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:48.490
It's a creative process,

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:51.490
so sometimes the best ideas come at you

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:52.630
when you're taking a shower.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:53.463
You know?

00:04:53.463 --> 00:04:54.653
Or driving.

